.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:35:- Doesn't learn from the correction to avoid the same mistake tomorrow
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:37:Every layer failed: resolution, context selection, correction handling, and learning.
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:112:| 1 | User corrections, user-authored fields | 1.0 | Direct intent — the user told us |
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:129:- User corrections: 365 days (long memory)
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:137:Track per-source, per-entity-type reliability using a Beta distribution. When a signal leads to a user correction, update the distribution:
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:220:3. Check for pattern: if the same correction happens N times for the same attendee group, auto-learn the mapping.
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:262:| **Phase 1: Foundation** | `signal_events` table, signal schema, basic Bayesian fusion, project keyword matching, re-enrichment on entity correction | `strsim` | 1 sprint |
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:263:| **Phase 2: Learning** | `signal_weights` table, Thompson Sampling, user correction feedback loop, confidence thresholds in hygiene | `rand_distr` | 1 sprint |
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:284:- **Cold start problem.** New users have no correction history, so learned weights default to static priors. The system only gets smart after the user interacts for a few weeks.
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:291:- **Learning rate.** With ~5-10 meetings per day and corrections on maybe 1-2, the Thompson Sampling weights converge slowly. May take weeks to learn meaningful signal preferences. Mitigate with reasonable static priors.
.docs/decisions/0080-signal-intelligence-architecture.md:302:**Rejected because:** The dataset is too small for reliable ML. 500-2000 meetings with sparse corrections means the model would overfit. Bayesian fusion with learned priors achieves similar accuracy with better interpretability and no training pipeline.
.docs/decisions/0096-glean-mode-local-footprint.md:50:- Stores user edits (field-level corrections persist across re-enrichment)
